## Meet my laptop!
![laptop behind banana](/resources/blog/banana-top.jpg)
"Wait that's not a laptop, that's just a banana!"

Look again, friend. Behind that razor-thin banana lays my ultra-slim 2008 Dell Latitude E6400.
![laptop next to banana](/resources/blog/banana-top-reveal.jpg)
*this is supposed to be a call-back to the [ipad air 2 "pencil" ad](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DZzJd57isdw) where they show off how thin the new ipad is by completely obscuring it behind a pencil.*   (maybe change this link to the cooler longer form ad that I like more instead of the short one?)

Handed down to me from my mother, I've had this laptop since I was around 12 years old. I played minecraft on it, I learned coding on it, and I watched YouTube on it. It was my childhood computer (and remains to be my primary laptop).

Ever since I built my first desktop tower I've only gone back to it when I *need* to take a computer with me. It's duel core processor along with only 4gb of ddr2 memory *is* fairly limiting.

## The start of my CD collection
I promise this ties back in!

At my place of work (a UPS store) we have speakers mounted on the ceiling. Unfortunately, they aren't hooked up to anything and we end up playing music off of a crappy alexa instead. Recently I discovered that we just have a 5 disc cd changer/radio/amp combo lying around. I also found the wires for the speakers hanging out the ceiling nearby. It seemed all I had to do to get it working was hook the wires up! I hooked it up, and the radio was working, but I didn't have any disks to use.

So I purchased some cheap inkjet printable disks, burned some music on them, and printed my own graphics on them!

![work safe music disc](/resources/blog/ups_disks.jpg)
The left disk is for after close only and contains more socially "objectionable" music. The right disk is for playing during normal operating hours and has all radio safe music.

Unfortunately, it turned out something about the cd player module is broken so it didn't end up working out. At least I got some fun discs out of it (not that I have a cd player to play them on...)

## My return to the laptop
Recently I decided to get my laptop set up again, that way it would be ready to go if I wanted to take it somewhere with me. After I got it set up, I sat on my bed and used it for a few hours working on my website.

The keyboard on the laptop is among one of my favorites, and it runs my code editor just fine (surprisingly a jetbrains ide is not too heavy for it). It's shockingly still perfectly fine for coding and other common tasks. Sitting somewhere else while using my computer felt really nice. I started to rediscover how great my old childhood laptop was
![laptop glamour shot](/resources/blog/laptop.jpg)

- mention playing back the music with the cd player
- mention keeping wifi off?

